course: dictionary
lesson: 6871
title: Overhead: above you, or a business cost
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Overhead means above your head or up in the sky. Read the clues around it: «Electricity from overhead railway wires can jump to you, so you never climb on anything near them.» The wires hang high above the track, so overhead must mean above.
explain: Overhead can work in three ways. As an adverb it tells you where something goes, as in «Geese flew overhead». As an adjective it describes a thing that is placed above you, as in «the overhead lights». In business, overheads is a noun: the regular costs of running a shop, like rent and electricity.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Does «Geese flew above in the sky» still make sense? Yes, so the adverb meaning fits. If your swapped meaning does not fit, try another meaning from the list.
fact: «Overhead» means above your head or up in the sky.
fact: Overhead wires in Britain carry about 25,000 volts, so never climb near them or fly a kite close to them.
fact: Electric trains take their power from overhead wires or from a third rail beside the track.
fact: On a plane, your bags go in the overhead lockers.
fact: «Overheads» means the regular costs of running a business, such as rent and electricity.
exercise: «Geese flew overhead as we walked home.» Where did the geese fly? | Under the ground | Behind the trees | In the car park | *Above us in the sky
exercise: In «an overhead light», what does overhead describe? | Sold cheaply | Very heavy | Made of wood | *Placed above you
exercise: «Never fly a kite near overhead wires.» Why? | Wires make kites go faster | *Electricity can jump from the wires to you | The wires are too thin to see | Kites are too heavy for the wind
test: In a plane, you put your bag in the ___ locker. | Back | Under | Front | *Overhead
test: In business, what are «overheads»? | The shop's shelves | *The regular costs of running the business, like rent | Old, unsold tickets | Free gifts for customers
exam: Which sentence uses overhead as an adverb meaning above? | «Overheads rose again this year.» | *«Geese flew overhead in a V shape.» | «Put your coat in the overhead locker.» | «The overhead light was broken.»
